<!-- ── §01 SCALING CEILING ── -->
<section id="s-ceiling" data-code="ceiling">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Scaling Ceiling</div>
<h2>Why bigger draft budgets stop helping</h2>
<p>Speculative decoding works by having a cheap draft model propose a batch of N tokens, then verifying all of them in one pass with the expensive target LLM. When verification passes, you commit multiple tokens per forward call — free throughput. The expected end-to-end speedup is governed by a simple formula:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Expected Speedup</div>
  <span class="math-var">S</span> = (1 − <span class="math-var">α</span><sup>N+1</sup>) / ((1 − <span class="math-var">α</span>) × (<span class="math-var">N</span> × <span class="math-var">c</span> + 1))
  <div style="margin-top:.5rem;font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted)">
    α = acceptance rate per token &nbsp;|&nbsp; N = draft length &nbsp;|&nbsp; c = drafter cost / target cost
  </div>
</div>

<p>Two parameters determine whether adding more draft tokens actually helps: <strong>α</strong> (how often the target model accepts a drafted token) and <strong>c</strong> (how expensive each draft token is relative to one target forward pass). The chart below shows how these interact — toggle between typical and ultra-low drafting cost to see the wall appear and disappear.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">expected speedup vs. draft budget N</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">toggle drafting cost</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ctrl-row">
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="btn-c-typical" onclick="setCost(0.05)">c = 0.05 (typical AR drafter)</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="btn-c-low" onclick="setCost(0.0005)">c = 0.0005 (ultra-low, block-parallel)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="ceiling-canvas" style="width:100%;display:block;border-radius:8px" height="260"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>At <strong>c = 0.05</strong> (an autoregressive drafter like EAGLE), speedup plateaus quickly around N = 16–32 regardless of α. The cumulative drafting overhead Nc cancels out the acceptance gains. At <strong>c = 0.0005</strong> (block-diffusion drafters like DFlash), speedup scales beautifully with budget — but only if α stays high. This is exactly the tension JetSpec has to resolve: you need both ultra-low cost <em>and</em> high acceptance rate simultaneously.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>The fundamental challenge:</strong> Block-diffusion drafters achieve ultra-low c by predicting all tokens in one pass — but without causal conditioning, their draft tokens are mutually inconsistent, dragging α down. Autoregressive drafters keep α high with causal conditioning — but their cost c scales with tree depth. No prior method solves both at once.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ── §02 THE DILEMMA ── -->
<section id="s-dilemma" data-code="dilemma">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Causality-Efficiency Dilemma</div>
<h2>Fast but inconsistent vs. consistent but slow</h2>

<p>When drafting a token tree, each branch represents a speculative continuation. For the target model to accept a branch, every token must be causally valid — each token should be conditioned on the actual tokens preceding it along that branch. Existing methods fail this in opposite directions:</p>

<div class="dilemma-grid">
  <div class="dilemma-card bad">
    <h4>✗ EAGLE / Autoregressive Drafters</h4>
    <p>Each depth requires a separate sequential draft pass. For a tree of depth 4, you run 4 serial forward calls through the draft head — cost grows linearly with depth.</p>
    <div class="dc-stat">cost c grows with tree depth</div>
  </div>
  <div class="dilemma-card bad">
    <h4>✗ DFlash / Block-Diffusion Drafters</h4>
    <p>All depths generated in one pass — but using <em>branch-agnostic marginals</em>. Tokens at each position are predicted independently of which token was chosen at earlier positions in this branch.</p>
    <div class="dc-stat">acceptance α hurt by inconsistency</div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>A concrete failure: the inconsistent tree</h3>
<p>On a real MATH-500 prompt, DFlash's block-diffusion head ranks the branch <em>"the given told that"</em> first — its combined marginal surrogate score is −3.76 nats. But when the target model evaluates this branch <em>causally</em> (conditioning each token on its actual predecessors), the true log-probability is <strong>−63.32 nats</strong>. A 59-nat gap. The tokens are individually plausible but mutually incompatible — "given" doesn't follow "the" in this context, and "told" doubly clashes. The actual best continuation <em>"are given that the"</em> sits at rank 3 with a target joint score of −0.08 nats. Budget is wasted drafting and verifying incoherent branches.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">branch-agnostic vs. causal tree scoring — real MATH-500 example</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">red = inconsistent (large surrogate↔target gap), green = consistent</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="tree-canvas" height="280"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Across 50 MATH-500 prompts, the diffusion head's rank-1 surrogate-to-target gap exceeds the causal head's on <strong>92% of prompts</strong>, with a median 5× larger gap (+62.81 nats vs +12.36 nats). This directly translates to fewer accepted tokens per verification step.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §03 ARCHITECTURE ── -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">03 — JetSpec Architecture</div>
<h2>One forward pass, branch-wise causality</h2>
<p>JetSpec's key insight is that you can have both: generate all tree-node logits in a single parallel forward pass, <em>while</em> enforcing branch-wise causal conditioning through a tree-causal attention mask. The mask is computed once per tree shape and applied to the draft head — zero extra sequential passes needed.</p>

