<p>The frozen target model provides hidden states from multiple intermediate layers. A feature fusion module concatenates and projects these into a compact representation. The causal parallel draft head then attends to this representation with a <strong>tree-causal attention mask</strong>: every tree node can attend to the original prefix and all its branch ancestors, but not to sibling branches or descendants. All tree-node logits are computed in one forward pass — no sequential depth unrolling needed.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Key mechanism:</strong> The tree-causal mask enforces that node v only attends to its ancestors Anc(v). This means <code>q(y_v | x, h_fused, π&lt;v)</code> — each node's distribution conditions on the actual tokens chosen along its branch, exactly matching the target model's autoregressive factorization. No extra sequential passes required.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ── §04 CAUSAL CONDITIONING ── -->
<section id="s-causality" data-code="causality">
<div class="section-no">04 — Causal Conditioning in Action</div>
<h2>How the mask eliminates the inconsistency gap</h2>
<p>The tree-causal attention mask induces a <em>branch-wise draft factorization</em> that mirrors the target model's autoregressive factorization. For each candidate branch, the joint draft probability is the product of per-node conditionals, where each node sees its own branch prefix — not a shared branch-agnostic distribution:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Branch-wise draft factorization (JetSpec)</div>
  q(π(v) | x) = ∏<sub>u ∈ π(v)</sub> q(y<sub>u</sub> | x, h<sub>fused</sub>, π<sub>&lt;u</sub>)
  <br/><span class="math-comment">← each node conditioned on its concrete branch ancestors π&lt;u</span>
  <br/><br/>
  <div class="math-label">Branch-agnostic surrogate (DFlash)</div>
  q<sub>sur</sub>(y<sub>1:k</sub> | x) ∝ ∏<sub>i</sub> r<sub>i</sub>(y<sub>i</sub> | x)
  <br/><span class="math-comment">← tokens at each depth predicted from shared branch-agnostic distribution</span>
</div>

<p>The canvas below visualizes the same decoding step with both approaches side by side — using real numbers from the MATH-500 failure example. The "surrogate score" is what the drafter uses to rank branches; the "target score" is what actually matters for acceptance. A large gap means wasted budget.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">causality gap: DFlash surrogate vs. target — real MATH-500 step 0</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag slider to reveal accepted tokens</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ctrl-row" style="justify-content:center;margin-bottom:1rem">
      <label style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2);display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.75rem">
        verification step:
        <input type="range" id="verify-slider" min="0" max="3" value="0" step="1" style="width:140px;accent-color:var(--purple)">
