</div>

<p>With JetSpec's causal conditioning, the rank-1 branch <em>"are told that"</em> has a surrogate-to-target gap of only <strong>−0.34 nats</strong>. The verification step accepts 6 tokens from this branch. DFlash's rank-1 branch <em>"the given told that"</em> has a gap of <strong>−59.56 nats</strong> — the target model immediately rejects it after the first token, accepting only 4 tokens total despite using the same draft budget.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── §05 BUDGET SCALING ── -->
<section id="s-scaling" data-code="scaling">
<div class="section-no">05 — Budget Scaling</div>
<h2>Turning larger budgets into actual speedup</h2>
<p>Because JetSpec simultaneously achieves ultra-low drafting cost c (one forward pass regardless of tree depth) and high acceptance rate α (causally consistent branches), it can convert larger draft budgets into longer accepted prefixes. The chart shows speedup on MATH-500 across budgets from 16 to 256 draft tokens — comparing EAGLE-3 (sequential AR), DDTree (DFlash head + tree), and JetSpec.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">speedup (×) vs. draft budget — MATH-500, Qwen3-8B, H100, temperature 0</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a method to highlight</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ctrl-row" id="chart-legend">
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="leg-eagle" onclick="setChartHL('eagle')">EAGLE-3</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="leg-ddtree" onclick="setChartHL('ddtree')">DDTree</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="leg-jetspec" onclick="setChartHL('jetspec')">JetSpec</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="leg-all" onclick="setChartHL('all')">all</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="scaling-canvas" style="width:100%;display:block;border-radius:8px" height="280"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>EAGLE-3's speedup barely grows past N=32 — the sequential drafting cost grows proportionally and throttles any acceptance gains. DDTree scales better (from 6.51× at 64 to 8.78× at 256) but is limited by its branch-agnostic draft head. JetSpec scales most strongly: <strong>6.76× → 8.93× → 9.64×</strong> at budgets 64 → 128 → 256, achieving 10% higher speedup than DDTree at the largest budget.</p>

<h3>Training data matters</h3>
<p>JetSpec uses target-model regenerated sequences for training — the draft head learns from the actual distribution it will be verifying against. Using the original training corpus instead ("JetSpec-Corpus") reduces speedup substantially at budget 256 (7.82× → 3.36× on GSM8K), confirming that matching the draft head to the target model's own generation style is critical for high acceptance rates.</p>
</section>

