<h3>MoE generalization</h3>
<p>On Qwen3-30B-A3B (a Mixture-of-Experts model), JetSpec maintains its advantage: <strong>9.45×</strong> speedup on MATH-500 vs DDTree's 8.61×, and <strong>10.65</strong> average accepted length τ vs 9.49. The causal tree drafting generalizes beyond dense transformers.</p>

<h3>Serving under load</h3>
<p>Integrated into vLLM, JetSpec's optimal budget depends on serving load. At batch size 1, budget 256 achieves <strong>968 tokens/sec</strong> and 6.75× speedup — budget 16 manages only 443 TPS and 3.09×. As batch size grows to 32, the gap narrows and budget 128 becomes more efficient due to lower per-step tree-verification overhead. This suggests serving-time dynamic budget scheduling as a promising future direction.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Why reverse-KL hurts:</strong> JetSpec uses forward-KL distillation. Reverse-KL (mode-seeking) concentrates draft probability mass too aggressively, causing a <strong>36–46% relative drop</strong> in speedup compared to forward-KL. Tree drafting benefits from covering the target model's full distribution, not just its mode — forward-KL preserves this coverage.
</div>

<!-- LISTEN-FRIENDLY CONTENT -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display:none" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: JetSpec — Breaking the Speculative Decoding Scaling Ceiling</h2>
    <p>Speculative decoding accelerates LLM inference by having a cheap draft model propose multiple tokens, then verifying them in parallel with the target model. The expected speedup depends on two variables: acceptance rate alpha and drafting cost c. Existing methods face a causality-efficiency dilemma — autoregressive drafters like EAGLE produce high-quality causally-conditioned drafts but cost scales with tree depth. Block-diffusion drafters like DFlash generate all tokens in one fast pass but produce mutually inconsistent branches, hurting acceptance rates. On a real MATH-500 example, DFlash's top-ranked branch had a surrogate score of minus 3.76 nats but a true target score of minus 63.32 nats — a 59-nat gap from inconsistent token combinations.</p>
    <p>JetSpec solves this with a tree-causal attention mask applied to a parallel draft head. All tree nodes are generated in one forward pass, but each node can only attend to its own branch ancestors, enforcing causal conditioning without sequential depth passes. This produces branch-wise draft distributions that mirror the target model's autoregressive factorization. At draft budget 256, JetSpec achieves 9.64x speedup on MATH-500 with Qwen3-8B on H100 GPUs, compared to 8.78x for DDTree and 8.33x for DFlash. The method generalizes to MoE models, achieves 9.45x on Qwen3-30B-A3B, and integrates into vLLM for real serving workloads.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：JetSpec — 突破推测解码的加速上限</h2>
    <p>推测解码通过让轻量级草稿模型提出多个候选词元，再由目标模型并行验证来加速大语言模型推理。加速效果取决于两个关键变量：词元接受率 alpha 和草稿生成成本 c。现有方法面临因果性与效率的两难困境：自回归草稿器（如 EAGLE）在因果一致性上表现良好，但生成成本随树深线性增长；块扩散草稿器（如 DFlash）虽能一次前向传播生成所有词元，却因忽略分支内部的因果依赖，导致候选词元相互矛盾，降低了接受率。在一个真实的 MATH-500 示例中，DFlash 排名第一的候选分支代理得分为 −3.76 奈特，但目标模型的真实得分为 −63.32 奈特，相差近 60 奈特。</p>
    <p>JetSpec 通过对并行草稿头施加树状因果注意力掩码来解决这一难题：所有树节点在一次前向传播中生成，但每个节点只能关注其所在分支的祖先节点，无需额外的顺序展开步骤。这使得草稿分布与目标模型的自回归分解保持一致。在草稿预算为 256 的情况下，JetSpec 在 Qwen3-8B 模型、H100 GPU 上的 MATH-500 基准测试中实现了 9.64 倍加速，超过 DDTree 的 8.78 倍和 DFlash 的 8.33 倍。该方法在混合专家模型上同样有效，并已成功集成至 vLLM 用于实际推理服务。</p>
  </div>
</div>

